# Intro
Two 29.7 Msun stars in a periodic box of molecular gas, with the ISRF transport on
(`ISRF_propagation: 1`) and the H2 line absorption of the transport on
(`ISRF_H2_line_absorption: 1`). A dense slab (density contrast 300 at a uniform H2 ratio)
shadows star A. Star B sits in clear gas on the other side of the slab. A reception slot
spans about 90 degrees, and two lights share a slot only where their directions fall in
the same slot. Where the two lights fall in different slots (the check verifies this and
voids the run otherwise), they do not interact: the field of the two-star run must equal
the sum of the fields of two runs with one star each. The check tests that, tests the
carried H2 column of each star against the straight ray through the slab, and tests the
column of the two-star run against the one-star columns.

Read the README of `ISRFH2StarLit` for the build, the radiation table, the parameters that
the run holds fixed and the exit classes.

# Run
```
./run.sh
leg=switch_off ./run.sh       # line absorption off: the check must stop on the missing line fields
leg=stale_key ./run.sh        # an old GeajayCooling:h2_transported_lw_rate_shielding on the command line has no effect
```
The main leg runs swift four times with one binary: star A alone, star B alone, star A
alone again, and both stars. Each run is kept in its own directory (`only_a`, `only_b`,
`only_a2`, `main`). A star keeps its particle ID in the one-star runs, because the ID seeds the
random frame of the emission pixels. `run.sh` reads the number of reception slots from
`config.h` next to the `swift` executable. `contrast` (default 300), `level`, `gas_density`,
`gas_mass`, `h2_ratio` and `n_threads` override the defaults.

# Checking
`checkH2Attenuation.py twosource` reads the first and last snapshot of the two-star run,
the last snapshots of the three one-star runs (`--only-a`, `--only-b`, `--repeat-a`, run
directories), `fixture.json`, the parameters, the logs and `--slots`. The reference is
snapshot 1, the first written after the first step.

Premises. The check is void (exit class 4) unless all of these hold:
- The four runs have the same parameters, the same build lines and the same slot frame in
  their logs, and the stars sit at the fixture positions with the IDs of the two-star run.
- The `n_H2` of every gas particle at the last snapshot lies inside the range of snapshot 1,
  slab gas and clear gas separately, up to 1e-3.
- At every particle lit by both stars, the light of the two stars sits in different slots.
  The check takes every periodic image of each star that lies within the reach of its light
  (the largest lit distance in the one-star run plus one hop `gamma h_max`) and compares the
  slot of the direction from each image. No lit particle may be within reach of two images
  of the same star, and some particle must be lit by both stars. The slot rule has a control:
  directions along `+x` share a slot, `-x` does not.
- Two identical runs of star A agree within the round-off bar below.

Checks:
- `LWOwnSpecificEnergies` and `LWOwnDissociatingSpecificEnergies` of the two-star run equal
  the sums of the one-star fields. A field is a sum of positive shares, so reordering the
  sum changes it by at most `n (k 2^-24 + p delta_h)` of its value (the bar of the
  two-rank check): `n` is the number of steps, `k` the largest number of neighbours in a
  kernel, `p` the cone power plus 4 and `delta_h` the largest relative gap of the smoothing
  lengths between the runs. The bar applies to the particles that held light at the
  previous snapshot. The others, the light front and dark gas, are held to the bar times
  the largest value in their kernel.
- The carried column `LWLineH2ColumnDensities` of each one-star run, at every lit particle,
  lies within `l_hop (n_H2 + TV)` of the straight-ray column from the star (nearest periodic
  image) through the slab and the clear gas. `l_hop` is `gamma h_max`, `n_H2` the particle's
  own H2 density, and `TV` the jump of `n_H2` at the slab faces times the number of faces
  within `l_hop` of the ray in `x` (the SPH density spreads a face over the kernel support).
  The sum check cancels an error that is common to all runs; this one does not. A column
  that is 1.5 times too large must fall outside the bar at some particle, else the run is
  void. The face term is set from the smoothing reach `gamma h_max`; it is not a proven
  bound for a ray that crosses a face at a slant. The reference sums the nominal H2 densities
  along the ray. It does not call the shielding function, so it detects no error in the
  shielding function that all four runs share; the star-lit check covers that.
- At every particle lit by both stars, the column of the two-star run equals the mean of
  the one-star columns weighted by their dissociating energies, `N_AB = (D_A N_A + D_B N_B)
  / (D_A + D_B)`, because the code stores `sum(D_s N_s) / sum(D_s)` over the slots. Both
  sums are made of positive shares. The round-off bar above covers the weights, and the
  ratio lies within twice that bar. The gap of the slot column between the runs is not
  bounded by that bar; it stayed below 0.02 of the bar on the default run. The identity
  needs `ISRF_packet_read_mean_time_Myr = 0` (the default); the gate reads it from
  `used_parameters.yml` and voids the run otherwise.

The check also prints, without gating, the largest change of the ratio `D / E` at the
particles lit by both stars and the largest change of `H2I` between the runs; the
dissociation of H2 by the other star is not part of the round-off bar.
